## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 44%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 23%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of molas, including the Mola Design Project examples from previous lessons. Students review colors and shapes by pointing and saying simple sentences. The teacher asks, "What colors does this mola have?" (e.g., "It has red, yellow, and blue.") and "What shapes do you see?" (e.g., "I see squares and circles."). This reactivates vocabulary from the theme "A Mola Has Many Colors!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a simple mola design. Teacher points to a mola picture and says, "Look at this mola. It is very colorful. It has a butterfly. First, I choose the yellow fabric. Then, I cut the round shape. I like this mola because it is vibrant." The teacher highlights the /m/ sound in 'mola' and the /k/ sound in 'colors' by exaggerating pronunciation. Teacher then models how to describe one step of the Mola Design Project. "First, I cut the fabric carefully."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I explaining my mola to a friend?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I using my own words to describe it?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated "Mola Design Project Steps" handout (short instructional text with pictures). The handout describes the creation of a Mola, using sequenced steps (First, Second, Third, Next, Last) and includes pictures of students performing these actions. Example steps: "First, choose your fabric colors. Second, cut shapes like squares or circles. Third, paste the shapes carefully. Next, decorate your mola. Last, express your creativity!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing project steps: Using instructional language to describe creative tasks, such as 'First, gather your materials.' Describing things: 'A mola has many colors.' Expressing preferences: 'I like this mola because it has many shapes.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the "Mola Design Project Steps" aloud, pointing to the pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match sentence strips of the steps to the correct picture (e.g., "Cut shapes" to a picture of scissors and fabric).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False questions: "First, you sew the fabric." (False, according to the example text where it's 'choose'). "You cut shapes." (True).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Subordinating conjunction 'because', Present simple ('A mola has...'), Instructional steps (First, Second, Third, Next, Last). Vocabulary: mola, creativity, project, culture, cut, sew, paste, choose, express, illustrate, decorate, collaborate, turquoise, golden, round, vibrant, traditional, unique, carefully, skillfully, artistically, also, and, because. Pronunciation: /m/ in 'mola', /k/ in 'colors'. Phonemic awareness: Spell one-syllable or two-syllable words (e.g., 'red,' 'orange,' 'vibrant,' 'second').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze & Spelling):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks related to the Mola Design Project steps and mola descriptions. Students fill in the blanks using word banks. E.g., "First, I ______ the fabric." (cut/paste). "A mola has many ______." (colors/shapes). "I like this mola ______ it is unique." (because/and). Students also spell key words like 'red', 'orange', 'vibrant', 'second' aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Sentence Building & Pronunciation):</b> In pairs, students receive flashcards with single words (e.g., 'mola', 'cut', 'red', 'because', 'first'). They take turns creating simple sentences using these words, focusing on instructional steps or describing molas. Partner A picks a card and says, "First, I choose the red fabric." Partner B repeats, focusing on /m/ and /k/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Mini-Explanation):</b> Students are given a simple drawing of a mola or a set of shuffled "Mola Project Step" picture cards. In pairs, Student A describes the mola's features ("This mola has squares and circles. It is colorful because it has turquoise and golden fabric.") or puts the step cards in order and explains the first two steps to Student B ("First, you choose colors. Next, you cut shapes."). Student B listens and checks for understanding.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Mola Project Explanation to an Absent Friend" – Students work in pairs. One student acts as the "expert" who created their "Mola Design Project" (from the 21st Century Skills Project focus). The other student acts as an "absent friend" who missed the previous lessons and needs to understand how to make a mola and what molas are about. The "expert" explains their mola project and the main ideas about molas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students retrieve their completed "Mola Design Project" (a drawing or simple craft).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, Student A explains their Mola Design Project to Student B. Student A explains the steps (e.g., "First, I chose the red fabric. Then I cut the round shape. Next, I pasted it carefully.") and describes the mola (e.g., "My mola has a turtle. It is vibrant because it has many colors: red, blue, and yellow."). Student A also explains what a mola is: "Molas are traditional art from Panama. They are unique."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B listens, asks clarifying questions (e.g., "What colors did you choose?", "Why did you cut a round shape?"), and tries to follow the instructions. Student B can also help count colors: "One, two, three colors!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students switch roles, allowing Student B to explain their project.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining, using instructional steps, describing colors and shapes, expressing preferences with 'because', and engaging in collaborative interaction by asking and answering questions, providing feedback (e.g., "Your mola is very creative!", "I like your color choices.").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete the "Mola Project Explanation" task (as described in Stage 4). The "absent friend" peer uses a simple checklist to assess the "expert's" explanation. The teacher also observes student interactions and explanations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My friend explained the mola steps (First, Next). (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My friend described colors and shapes. (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My friend said "because" to explain why they like their mola. (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I understood my friend's explanation. (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My friend used words like 'mola', 'cut', 'colorful'. (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can explain <u>two steps</u> of my mola project.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say <u>two colors and shapes</u> in my mola.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy when you explained your mola project to your friend?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was a little difficult when you explained it?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The mediation task was successful in allowing students to integrate learned vocabulary and grammar to explain their project. The use of visuals (their own Mola Design Projects) strongly supported their explanations. Some students needed more prompting for specific instructional language (e.g., 'Next' vs. just 'then'). The pronunciation of /m/ and /k/ was generally clear, but some struggled with spelling two-syllable words. For future lessons, I need to provide more sentence frames for complex explanations (using 'because') and continue emphasizing the sequence adverbs for clearer project descriptions. Collaborative feedback was positive. This final lesson effectively consolidated the theme of "A Mola Has Many Colors!" and the Project Focus.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "A Mola Has Many Colors!". The next unit will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on the foundational skills of explaining and describing learned here.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>